README_X.1ST

			   MCNPX 2.4.0
	     Official MCNPX Release date August 1, 2002

		   RSICC identifier C00715MNYCP00

This distribution CD includes the LANL MCNPX code system plus 
MCNPXDATA cross sections for Unix operating systems.  In RSICC's 
collection, the packages are identified as C00715MNYCP00 MCNPX 2.4.0 
and D00205ALLCP03 MCNPXDATA. This release is for Unix, Linux or
Windows operating systems.  


WinZIP 8.0 or later is required to expand the distribution tar
files on Windows.

The GZIP and GMAKE utilities are required for MCNPX installation 
and may be downloaded through the web. A GNU make.exe file is
included in the Windows distribution. If you do not have gmake, you
may download it ftp://ftp.gnu.org/pub/gnu/.  Click on the applications 
you need (i.e. gzip or make), then select and save the versions you want.


Documentation
-------------

Installation instructions will be extracted to 
	/mcnpx_2.4.0/Docs/Install.html.

The DOC subdirectory on the distribution CD contains 2 files: 
C715.PDF and D205.PDF.

C715.PDF includes 2 LANL reports on mcnpx2.4.0: 
"MCNPX User's Manual, Version 2.4.0,"  LA-CP-02-408  (September, 2002).
L. S. Waters, ed., “MCNPX User’s Manual, Version 2.3.0,” LA-UR-02-2607 (April 2002)

Various reports and information files for the MCNPXDATA package 
are included in the D205.PDF file in the DOC subdirectory on CD.
Readme files will be expanded to various subdirectories in your 
mcnpx_2.4.0 directory.


You must read the Install.html file before building mcnpx.
The mcnpx report also includes installation information.
The scripts and make files are configured to run in the Bourne shell.  
They can also run in the ksh and bash shells on some systems.


Graphics
--------

The only graphics support for this release is X11 
http://www.x.org/Downloads_terms.htm.

If graphics are desired, an X-Server is required.   
Several flavors can be found on the internet 
(e.g., X-Deep/32, WRQ Reflection X, Exceed, etc.). 

The mcnpx_2.4.0/README.w32 text file contains 
information on downloading and customizing for 
Windows. The  x11.dll binary file is included 
for Windows users and will be expanded by the 
build procedure from mcnpx_2.4.0/Win32.exe.  
X11 is generally included on Unix and Linux systems. 


Sample install
--------------

An example of the commands RSICC used to run mcnpx on a Linux PC are 
listed below. These commands build mcnpx, run test cases, and store 
exes and libraries in the /home/uid/Build/bin and /home/uid/Build/lib 
directories. If you do not specify "--prefix", the "gmake install" 
procedure saves them in /usr/local. In this example, the Portland 
Group Fortran 90 and GNU CC compiler are specified.

cd /home/uid
gzip -dc C715TAR0.GZ | tar xf - 
mkdir Build		(create dir in which to build the code system)
cd Build
../mcnpx_2.4.0/configure --prefix=/home/uid/Build --with-FC=pgf90 --with-CC=gcc
gmake all	(build mcnpx exe and libraries)
		(executable will be in file /home/uid/Build/src/mcnpx)
gmake tests	(run tests in /home/uid/Build/src/Test)
gmake install	(store exes and libraries and clean up)


The "gmake tests" step runs test cases and writes results 
and dif* files in the Build/src/Test directory. The diff 
comparisons are run only if a user's configuration matches 
one of the configurations provided. 


Included executables
--------------------

The LANL developers ran MCNPX 2.4.0 on the following systems. 
They provided  executables included in mcnpx_2.4.0/exe. 
Compilation may fail with different compilers.
LANL results for these systems in mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test

Sun-Solaris/WorkShop Fortran Compilers 6, update 2 (Fortran 95 6.2)
SGI-IRIX/MIPSpro Compilers: Version 7.30 under 64 bit IRIX & 32 bit IRIX
HP-HPUX/HP F90 v2.4.10
IBM-AIX/xlf90 Version 7 Release 1
DEC Alpha-Tru64 running OSF1 V5.0 with Compaq Fortran V5.3-915
Intel-Linux 7 with The Portland Group Fortran Group, Inc. f90 3.2-3
Windows2000 on Pentium IV - Compaq Visual Studio 6.6 and Microsoft C++ 6.0
(Note that Compaq Visual Studio 6.5 fails to compile the code, but 6.1 works.)

See User's Notes in Install.html for information on how to run 
test cases with the executables contained in the mcnpx distribution.


DATA libraries
--------------

Cross section libraries are in the DLC-200/MCNPXDATA package
(RSICC ID: D00205ALLCP03), which includes data for previous mcnp
releases plus the LA150 libraries and the latest electron data 
file (EL03) which requires an MCNP4C based code. The cross 
sections will expand and create a directory called "mcnpxdata" 
under your current directory.

Other LAHET data are distributed in the CCC-715 distribution.
The ASCI version of the original PHTLIB released with 
MCNPX 2.1.5 is distributed in mcnpx_2.4.0/Data/LCS/bcdlib.
The build procedure will create a binary phtlib file from
the bcdlib file using the hcnv program.

An alternate version of PHTLIB is now available.  
More information is included in the MCNPX2.3.0 report. 
See Section 4.3.1.5 on page 54 of LA-UR-02-2607. The 
alternate data are included in this distribution in 
mcnpx_2.4.0/Data/LCS/bcdlib.spec1l.


The MCNPXDATA Read*.txt files contain brief descriptions 
of data libraries that are distributed with the cross sections 
in the mcnpxdata subdirectory. See the revised Appendix G of 
the MCNP manual which is included in the RSICC documentation 
for DLC-205 (file DOC/D205.PDF on the distribution CD.) 
Readme_x.txt includes some general info about the MCNPXDATA 
distribution, and Readme_la150* files include information on 
the new LA150 libraries. Readme_4c.txt and Readme_endf60.txt 
were copied from the DLC-200 distribution.


Expanding the code system requires ~250 MB, and about another 
125MB is required to build executables and run test cases. The 
cross section libraries require ~1.2 GB of hard disk space. 
The makxs utility is built by the mcnpx install procedure. 
It can be used to convert ASCII cross sections to binary mode 
which saves space and enables mcnpx to run faster.

